{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/12/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"99caaf76\" lines=\"56-75\">\n<description>\nMove each marked cross object away from its marker to the grid edge while rotating the colored cross 180 degrees.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each 8-connected foreground object has nine pixels: a 4-pixel marker-color\n  sprite attached to a 5-pixel non-marker colored cross.\n- Each object bbox is either 3x6 or 6x3, so the marker sprite is on exactly\n  one side of the cross.\n\nRule:\n- Move the whole object straight away from its marker side until the object\n  bbox touches the corresponding grid edge.\n- Keep the marker sprite unchanged within the object.\n- Rotate the 3x3 colored cross block by 180 degrees before placing it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker sprite is a directional tail; the cross is the leading payload,\nand only that payload turns around during the slide to the edge.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25c199f5\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nPack the non-background rows from three separated panels into one panel, scanning panels right-to-left.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid contains three equal-size panels separated by two full-height divider columns.\n- The background is the dominant color; divider pixels use a color that appears only in the divider columns.\n\nRule:\n- Read panels from right to left, and within each panel read rows from top to bottom.\n- Keep each row that contains any non-background pixel, discarding all-background rows.\n- Place the kept rows at the bottom of a single panel-shaped output, padding above with background rows.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is row packing: the divider columns mark independent panels, and only the occupied rows are moved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b8318e3\" lines=\"86-105\">\n<description>\nMove each isolated colored pixel into the one-cell frame around a grey 2x2 block.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background is the dominant color.\n- Grey foreground objects are solid 2x2 anchor blocks.\n- Every other non-background, non-grey cell is a colored singleton source.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the grey anchors fixed and clear all colored singleton source pixels.\n- For each singleton and each anchor, the normal destination is the cell reached by\n  clamping the pixel into the anchor's one-cell outer frame.\n- If a diagonally outside singleton has an exact 8-direction sightline into an anchor,\n  also allow the cell just before the first grey pixel on that ray.\n- Choose distinct destinations that minimize total king-move distance; at equal\n  distance prefer exact sightlines, then the shorter Manhattan movement.\n\nKey insight: The grey 2x2 blocks define magnetic frames; conflicts are resolved\nstructurally by nearest-frame assignment rather than by color identity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db118e2a\" lines=\"7-23\">\n<description>\nNormalize the framed input motif into a 5x5 icon and stamp two copies on a 15x15 canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is a rounded rectangular frame: the four corners are the background\ncolor, and every other outer-border pixel has one frame color. Non-background\npixels inside that frame are the motif to preserve.\n\nRule\nMake a 5x5 version of the rounded frame with a 3x3 interior. Crop the input\ninterior motif to its bounding box, center that crop in the 3x3 interior, and\npaste the resulting 5x5 icon at upper-left positions (2, 2) and (8, 8) in a\n15x15 background canvas.\n\nKey insight\nThe variable-size frame is only a carrier; the meaningful object is the\ncompact interior motif, which is normalized to the fixed 3x3 icon interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5d588b4d\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.\n- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.\n\nRule:\n- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths\n  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.\n- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.\n- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row\n  with background pixels.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length\ndiamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8abad3cf\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRepack each non-background color group as a solid square ordered by increasing size.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one dominant background color, and each other color has a\nperfect-square pixel count. Those square side lengths are unique.\n\nRule\nConvert every non-background color into a filled square whose side length is\nthe square root of its pixel count. Place the squares from left to right in\nincreasing side length, bottom-aligned, with one background column between\nneighboring squares.\n\nKey insight\nThe original geometry of each color is discarded; only the color's total\npixel count determines the size of its packed output square.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nRecover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n\nRule:\n- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n\nKey insight:\nThe hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"87ab05b8\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nErase all distractor pixels and expand the single red control pixel into the grid quadrant that contains it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Exactly one red pixel is the control pixel.\n- Other non-background pixels are distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n- Split the grid into equal top/bottom and left/right halves.\n- Fill the quadrant containing the red control pixel with red.\n\nKey insight:\n- The red control pixel selects a whole half-grid quadrant by its row half and column half; all other foreground pixels are noise.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b80bb43\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nRemove diagonal staircase noise and complete broken straight lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; one foreground color fg forming axis-aligned lines.\n- Some fg pixels form 45° diagonal chains (8-connected but 4-disconnected from lines).\n\nRule:\n- Delete all diagonal-only fg pixels (those with no orthogonal fg neighbor, only diagonal ones).\n- Fill short gaps in horizontal/vertical lines where a deleted diagonal was nearby.\n- Prune remaining diagonal-adjacent leaf pixels near former diagonal sites.\n\nKey insight: Diagonal pixels are hints marking where a straight line is broken; their span sets the maximum gap size to fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\" lines=\"71-87\">\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83b6b474\" lines=\"95-111\">\n<description>\nTranslate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.\n\nInput structure\nA grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic\n4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line\nsprites.\n\nRule\nPreserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and\nplace the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular\nframe exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count\ndetermines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame\nassembly with the least difference between height and width.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4a1cacc2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nFill the rectangle from the lone foreground pixel to its nearest grid corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a uniform background field with exactly one foreground pixel.\n- The foreground pixel's color is the color used for all added pixels.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
